Per-market learning needs more than a win/loss counter

Each demo run can contribute training data for its selected markets. However, we should store the circumstances surrounding decisions, not just whether completed trades won.

Record	What it should capture
Market observation	Timestamp, price data, calculated features, and source
Decision	Enter, wait, or reject—with the reason
Proposed trade	Direction, product, duration, quote, and expected return
Executed trade	Entry, settlement, costs, outcome, and strategy version
Training run	Dataset period, model settings, results, and resulting version

This matters particularly for learning when not to enter. Executed trades alone cannot show whether rejected opportunities would have performed better or worse. We can record hypothetical outcomes for skipped opportunities separately; those must never appear as actual trades or account profit.

The existing local SQLite database is a useful foundation. Its current recent-200-trade statistic would need a separate cumulative eligible-trade counter for your 500-trade baseline.

Training data must also preserve the distinction between:

Fictional simulator outcomes.
Live-price paper trades.
Deriv virtual-account trades.

Fictional random-walk trades should not qualify a real-market model as trained. Replaying the same dataset repeatedly should not inflate the count of unique training examples.

Use 500 trades as a minimum baseline, with validation tracked separately

Your threshold is workable as a product rule. I would display these states:

State	Meaning
Default algorithm	No eligible market-specific training history
Collecting data: 240/500 — 48%	Partially complete baseline
Baseline complete	At least 500 eligible trades; training or validation still pending
Trained—validation pending	A fitted model exists but has not passed evaluation
Validated model available	The model passed the defined evaluation checks
Review required	Results have deteriorated or compatibility has changed

Five hundred trades do not establish that a strategy is profitable. Validation should examine net results, drawdown, consistency across periods, and performance on later data excluded from training. Features must contain only information available at the decision time; otherwise future information can leak into training and exaggerate performance.

The baseline should apply to a compatible profile: market + product + duration/timeframe + strategy version. Five hundred short-duration Rise/Fall trades should not automatically qualify a CFD strategy.

The default algorithm can operate under the existing risk controls while collecting evidence. It must remain free to wait; reaching 500 trades should never become a reason to force entries.

Downloadable and uploadable algorithms are feasible

A dedicated Market Algorithms section could show each market’s profiles, training progress, validation status, active version, and export/import controls.

An exported bot package should include:

Market and product compatibility.
Strategy version and configurable parameters.
Feature definitions, preprocessing, and learned model state.
Training counts, dates, provenance, and validation results.
Required app/model format versions.

That package can perform inference on another compatible installation without the original training database. However, independently reproducing its training results would require the underlying data.

An imported profile should distinguish its reported history from local history—for example:

Imported model: 820 source training trades · 0 local trades

It should not fabricate 820 local database records. Partially trained checkpoints can also be exported, retaining their incomplete status. Resuming training may require additional training state, depending on the algorithm.

Per-market configuration should sit beneath the shared risk engine

Each market’s trader section can offer:

Configuration area	Examples
Algorithm	Default rules, trained model, imported profile, custom script
Product	Supported Rise/Fall or a future Multipliers/CFD adapter
Parameters	Indicator periods, entry thresholds, confidence thresholds
Execution	Contract duration, requested stake, cooldown, trading hours
Learning	Training schedule, dataset window, candidate model settings
Lifecycle	Active version, previous version, validation status, rollback

Global allocation, floor, exposure, and cruise limits must remain authoritative. A market profile can request a trade; it cannot override those limits.

Custom Python uploads are possible, but they introduce executable code into the PC. I would put that feature in an advanced section, with explicit activation, restricted operating-system isolation, resource limits, and no broker credentials. Scripts should return signals to the main engine rather than place orders themselves. A separate Python process alone is insufficient isolation.

How this changes the earlier update priorities

Stage	Recommended scope
Next: market workspace	Curated line-up, five mini-graphs, click-through observation, source-backed suggestions, market profiles, richer database logging, and training progress
Then: research and learning	Historical backtesting, strategy comparison, per-market training, chronological validation, versioning, and rollback
Then: portability	Export/import of trained profiles and partial checkpoints, compatibility checks, and imported-model status
Additional execution and extensions	Multipliers support for Crash/Boom, isolated custom Python scripts, and external notifications

My recommendation is to build the market workspace and training-data foundation first. That gives subsequent learning and portable models the evidence and compatibility controls they need, while preserving your existing capital protection rules.

