Also show net profit, drawdown, and average win versus average loss alongside win rate. For example, 90 wins earning $0.10 each and 10 losses costing $1 each produce a 90% win rate but a $1 loss.

Named bots and user-selected training data

The proposed creation process is practical:

Select a market.
Name the bot—for example, EUR/USD Session Filter.
Choose an algorithm template or compatible imported model.
Select its product, timeframe/duration, and configurable parameters.
Select all compatible market data or a subset.
Train and evaluate a candidate version.
Select it for forward demo trading.

Useful dataset filters would include:

Filter	Purpose
Date range	Test recent conditions or a broader history
Trading session	Examine time-of-day differences
Product and duration	Keep the training relevant to the intended trades
Data source	Distinguish historical replay, paper demo, broker demo, and live
Market conditions	Study volatility, trend, or other recorded conditions
Data quality	Exclude incomplete, duplicate, or unreliable records

Win-only or loss-only datasets should be available for diagnosis, but not presented as balanced training evidence. They omit part of the decision problem.

A separate evaluation period should remain outside the training selection. Time-ordered evaluation is important because ordinary random splits can allow a model to learn from future observations while being evaluated on earlier ones.

A newly created bot must earn its own performance record

Suppose the PC contains 1,000 EUR/USD trades collected by the default algorithm. A new named bot may use compatible records from that dataset.

Its display could then read:

Training records: 1,000 / 500 baseline met
Historical validation: 58% · 180 evaluated trades
Forward demo: 0% · 0 settled trades

It must not inherit the default bot’s win rate. Its historical evaluation must replay its own decisions against the market data.

This makes storing price history, decision-time features, quotes, and skippedtrading version should remain identifiable and stable while its replacement is being evaluated. Updating a model should not alter an already-open trade’s management.

Live and demo outcomes should retain separate labels and performance figures. Combining their training data can be an explicit option, because execution and settlement differences can affect results.

For every version, preserve:

Which data trained it.
Which data evaluated it.
Its configuration and creation date.
Trades actually attributed to that version.
The previous version for rollback.

Your 500-trade requirement remains a minimum training-data baseline, not an automatic certificate of readiness. Retraining the same records repeatedly does not increase the number of unique training records.

How I would prioritize the expanded requirements

Priority	Update	Why it comes here
1 — Foundation	Named bot registry, version identifiers, richer market/decision database, accurate per-bot metrics	Everything else depends on correct attribution and sufficient data
2 — User controls	Market mini-graphs, training progress, per-market bot selection and configuration	Makes the system understandable and manageable
3 — Research	Dataset selection, historical replay, chronological validation, strategy comparison	Establishes how candidates will be judged
4 — Learning	Per-market training, constrained parameter tuning, candidate comparison and rollback	Adds adaptation to an established evaluation process
5 — Portability	Export/import models, configurations, provenance, and reported performance	Allows reuse without fabricating local trading history
6 — Advanced execution	Additional product adapters, isolated custom Python scripts, then separately validated live trading	Requires the earlier controls to be dependable

Several named bots can research or run in shadow mode for the same market. Initially, I recommend one executing bot per selected market, with all five markets sharing your existing capital controls. This lets you compare algorithms without accidentally multiplying account exposure.

The next substantive upgrade should therefore combine the bot registry, per-bot performance tracking, richer data collection, and the market workspace. Those provide the foundation for meaningful training rather than simply a rising trade counter.

Let's further expand on the future updates potential:

How about we establish a standard for portable "setup" files. Users will be able to buy and use as is, or configure these setup files for each market. They may also sell these files back to other users with their boasted success rates. These files are uploadable into the app as a proprietary file type.

We should have several categories of setup files:

bot - for full setup, including bot, algorithm and all variable configurations set.
setup - configuration data for bots
training - training to update a bot's configuration based on successful training on the data

These files should have a standard format, that is also easy for a general user to fill/modify. Training data should be csv format.

Yes. A standard package format would make bots easier to configure, transfer, update, and eventually sell. I would establish the format before building a marketplace, so every installation interprets the files consistently.

The key distinction is between a complete bot, its settings, a trained update, and the raw data used for training.

Proposed file categories

The extensions below are working names:

Category	Proposed extension	Contents	Import behaviour
Bot	.tbot	Algorithm, learned model where applicable, configuration, market compatibility, documentation, and performance evidence	Creates a new bot or installs a specific version
Setup	.tsetup	Configuration values for an identified compatible algorithm	Previews changes to an existing bot; does not replace its algorithm
Training update	.ttrain	Learned model/state and configuration changes produced by training, plus evaluation results and provenance	Creates a candidate updated version of a compatible bot
Training data	.csv	Market observations, decisions, trades, outcomes, or other supported training records	Adds eligible records to the local database; training is a separate action

A CSV dataset and a trained update are different deliverables. Users could import CSV data to train their own bots, or import a .ttrain package containing the result of someone else’s training.

A training update should identify the algorithm and version it requires. It must not silently apply to an incompatible bot.

One shared format underneath the three package types

I recommend a ZIP-based container with the proprietary extension, containing:

Component	Purpose
Manifest	Package type, standard version, identity, author, and compatibility
Configuration	Readable settings and their values
Algorithm/model	Required implementation or learned state
Performance report	Results, evaluation conditions, and supporting evidence
Documentation	Description and instructions
Licence	Editing, sharing, commercial-use, and resale permissions
Integrity information	Detects changes and, where signed, identifies the publisher

This gives users a recognizable application-specific file while keeping the underlying structure portable.

A proprietary extension does not itself provide encryption, copy protection, or exclusive ownership. Those would be separate commercial decisions.

Make configuration easy through the app

General users should mainly work through an editor, rather than manually editing package internals.

The editor could provide:

A plain-language description beside each setting.
Supported values, defaults, and validation.
Basic and advanced views.
A preview of changes before applying them.
Save setup, Export bot, and Restore previous version actions.

For users who prefer manual editing, the configuration can remain readable JSON. For example:

{
  "format_version": "1.0",
  "package_type": "setup",
  "name": "EUR/USD Session Setup",
  "market": "frxEURUSD",
  "requires_algorithm": {
    "id": "session-momentum",
    "version": "1.2"
  },
  "settings": {
    "duration_seconds": 300,
    "fast_period": 10,
    "slow_period": 40,
    "cooldown_seconds": 120
  }
}

The application would validate this against its registered algorithm definitions. A seller cannot make an invalid setting acceptable merely by declaring it valid in the file.

Performance belongs to the exact configuration that produced it

This is particularly important if users will sell setups with advertised success rates.

A reported rate should identify:

Required context	Example
Market and product	EUR/USD, Rise/Fall
Algorithm and configuration version	Version 1.2, configuration fingerprint
Evidence source	Historical validation, forward demo, or live
Evaluation period	Start and end dates
Sample size	620 settled trades
Outcome counts	Wins, losses, and break-even trades
Financial results	Net trading profit, average win/loss, and drawdown
Assumptions	Payouts, costs, execution method, and exclusions

Changing performance-relevant settings creates a new configuration version. The original results remain visible as historical evidence, but they do not become the new configuration’s own results.

For example:

Purchased setup: Seller reports 68% across 620 demo trades.
Buyer’s installation: Local demo 0% across 0 settled trades.
Buyer modifies its entry threshold: New configuration—no evaluated results yet.

This prevents users from modifying a successful setup and continuing to advertise the original rate as though the modified version achieved it.

Separate claims from verification

I would use explicit evidence labels:

Label	What it establishes
Seller-reported	The publisher supplied the figures
Recomputed from supplied records	The app reproduced the arithmetic from the included records
Reconciled with broker records	Applicable trade records were matched against broker information
Locally evaluated	This installation generated the displayed results

Recomputing a CSV does not prove that its trades occurred or that the dataset includes every loss. A signature establishes publisher identity and file integrity; it does not establish profitability.

Therefore, editable descriptions may contain a seller’s claim, but the app’s calculated performance fields should be generated from identified evidence. No package should receive a blanket “verified winning bot” badge.

Training CSV standards should support different record types

One enormous CSV template would be difficult for general users to manage. I recommend separate templates sharing common identifiers.

CSV template	Principal fields
Prices	Market, UTC timestamp, price or OHLC values, timeframe, source
Trades	Trade ID, bot/version, market, product, direction, entry/exit times, stake, costs, net profit, mode
Decisions	Decision ID, market, timestamp, algorithm version, enter/wait/reject, reason, feature values

Some products require additional fields: an option needs its duration and quoted payout; a CFD needs position size, entry/exit prices, and relevant charges.

The importer should validate dates, units, duplicates, missing values, market identity, and product compatibility. It should show accepted and rejected row counts before committing the import.

Your 500-trade baseline should count eligible unique records, not repeated imports of the same trades. Imported market data also must not alter account balances or appear as newly executed trades.

Packages must not override the account’s controls

A purchased bot can arrive fully configured and ready for demo evaluation. Importing it should still require reviewing its market, configuration, and permissions.

The following should remain outside portable packages:

API tokens and account credentials.
Account balances and personal information.
Authorization to use live money.
Permission to weaken the global floor or exposure limits.

A bot can propose orders; the app’s central risk engine decides whether they are permitted.

Executable algorithms also need a separate trust boundary. Python’s own documentation warns that formats such as pickle can execute arbitrary code, and CPython does not provide an untrusted-code sandbox. A renamed file extension does not address that risk.

For the first package standard, I recommend supporting registered built-in algorithms and controlled model formats. Arbitrary Python plugins should follow later with operating-system isolation.

Selling and reselling need explicit permissions

Being able to edit a purchased setup should not automatically grant permission to resell it.

Each package should state whether it permits:

Personal use.
Modification.
Redistribution.
Resale of the original.
Commercial distribution of derivatives.

Modified packages should retain their parent package’s identity and author attribution. Their performance records should identify the modified version separately.

Licensing information can travel with an offline file, but enforcement against copying is a separate problem. A future marketplace can add publisher identities, purchase entitlements, listings, and dispute handling.

Revised priorities

Priority	Deliverable
1	Standard identifiers, bot/configuration versions, database attribution, and performance definitions
2	Readable configuration schema, editor, .tsetup import/export, and CSV templates
3	Per-market training, validation, and compatible .ttrain updates
4	Complete .tbot packages, provenance, signatures, and permission controls
5	Marketplace, publisher reputation, purchases, and resale workflows
6	Isolated custom Python plugins and stronger commercial protection

I would make Trading Package Standard 1.0 the next design specification. It should define the three package categories together, while implementing setup files and CSV imports first. That gives future training, full bot exports, and sales a consistent foundation.

Show the prioritized list of updates and their corresponding app version proposed

The current release is v1.1. I propose the following roadmap, with reliable data and evaluation established before automated learning or live trading.

Priority	Proposed version	Main updates
1	v1.2 — Market Workspace	Curated eight-market line-up; select up to five; live mini-graphs with abbreviated names; click to expand in Market Observation; source-backed market suggestions with timestamps and availability checks. Unsupported products remain observation-only.
2	v1.3 — Bot Manager & Performance	Create and name market-specific bots; select one executing bot per market; per-bot configuration and version history; separate historical, demo, and live win rates; sample counts, net profit, drawdown, and 500-trade baseline progress.
3	v1.4 — Data & Setup Standard	Richer local database for observations, decisions, trades, and provenance; validated CSV imports/exports; dataset selection; Trading Package Standard 1.0; configuration editor and .tsetup import/export.
4	v1.5 — Backtesting & Validation	Historical replay; strategy comparison; chronological training/validation/test separation; cost-aware evaluation; explicit readiness criteria and reproducible performance reports.
5	v1.6 — Market-Specific Learning	Train named bots on compatible market datasets; constrained configuration optimization; default algorithms until trained; candidate-versus-active model comparison; deterioration detection and rollback.
6	v1.7 — Portable Bots & Training	Complete .tbot packages and .ttrain updates; compatibility checks; imported-versus-local performance labels; provenance, integrity checks, and licence permissions.
7	v1.8 — Expanded Execution & Protection	Multipliers adapter for eligible markets, including Crash/Boom; product-specific sizing and recovery; configurable daily loss, losing-streak, and profit-protection controls; combined exposure testing.
8	v1.9 — Remote Management	External approval requests and alerts; connection monitoring; backup/restore; operating-health dashboard; optional hosted deployment.
9	v2.0 — Controlled Live Trading	Explicit live-account activation; broker reconciliation and recovery validation; separate live metrics; live-data collection and candidate training; deployment approval and rollback controls.
10	v2.1 — Advanced Strategy Plugins	Isolated custom Python algorithms; approved dependencies; resource limits; controlled access to market data; signals routed through the central risk engine.
11	v2.2 — Marketplace	Publisher profiles; package purchases and permitted resale; version-specific performance disclosures; evidence labels; entitlements, ratings, and dispute workflows.

Release gates matter more than version numbers: 500 eligible trades establishes the training baseline, validation determines model readiness, and live trading requires its own execution checks. Marketplace performance claims should depend on the evidence standards introduced earlier.

Let's discuss these additional features before including in the list of prioritized updates:
Include a feature that allows the user to connect their Deric account to switch to live trading in real markets, with sufficient warnings and verifications before switching.

Yes—assuming you mean Deriv. This would expand the proposed v2.0 Controlled Live Trading feature, rather than create a separate roadmap item. I would call it Deriv Account Connection & Live Activation.

The app should distinguish three modes clearly:

Mode	Prices	Money used
Live-price paper demo	Current market data	Fictional local funds
Deriv account demo	Broker market data	Deriv virtual funds
Live trading	Broker market data	Real account funds

Connecting an account must not automatically authorize trading. Deriv supports OAuth authorization without sharing the user’s password, as well as Personal Access Tokens. I would prefer a Connect with Deriv authorization flow for the finished app, subject to the application’s registration and integration requirements.

Proposed activation process

Connect and identify the account. Display the selected account, currency, balance, and whether it is real or virtual. Initially permit account inspection only.
Pause demo entries. For the initial implementation, require existing demo positions to settle before switching modes. Retain their history separately.
Select the live markets and bots. Check account-specific product availability, minimum stakes, durations, permissions, and current quotes.
Approve live capital and cycle length. Show the opening floor, permitted loss allowance, stake limits, and aggregate exposure across all selected markets.
Review warnings and verification results.
Explicitly activate live trading. Use a clearly labelled action such as “Enable real-money trading”, followed by the normal Start entries control.

Deriv provides separate public, demo, and real trading endpoints, so the application can enforce this distinction in its connection and execution logic.

Warnings should be specific to the proposed allocation

For a $50 allocation, the confirmation should explain:

Real money is at risk.
The opening protective floor is $40, representing a $10 planned loss allowance.
The floor rises under your agreed rules; it is not insurance against execution, broker, or software failures.
Demo and historical results do not guarantee live results.
Which markets, bot versions, and settings will receive permission to trade.

The warning should appear on first activation and when material exposure changes—not before every ordinary trade.

Verification should produce clear outcomes

Check	Proposed outcome if unsuccessful
Account identity or real/demo status cannot be established	Block activation
Insufficient funds or missing trading permission	Block activation
Unsupported market, product, duration, or stake	Disable the affected selection
Unresolved orders or broker/local-ledger mismatch	Block new entries until reconciled
Selected bot has not passed the agreed validation requirements	Block automatic live execution
Market data is stale or the connection is unhealthy	Block new entries

Your 500-trade baseline should remain part of bot readiness, alongside validation. A high advertised win rate or imported “trained” label alone should not satisfy live activation.

Several switching rules need to be explicit

Demo approvals do not carry over into live mode. Real capital requires separate authorization.
The $30 fallback must not activate real trading for the first time. During live onboarding, the user can separately enable that fallback for later cycle renewals. Otherwise, an unanswered live renewal pauses entries.
Switching back to demo does not close real positions. Pause live entries, show outstanding positions, and continue their defined management until settled or deliberately closed where supported.
Disconnecting or closing the app does not remove broker exposure. Show outstanding positions before disconnecting.
Restarting restores live position monitoring, with new entries paused. It should not silently switch an existing real account into a simulated ledger.

A persistent REAL MONEY indicator, selected account identifier, and visible pause control should remain on screen whenever live execution is enabled.

Live learning can continue without changing the active bot unexpectedly

Live trades would be recorded in the native database and attributed to their exact bot version. They could train candidate updates, while the executing version remains stable. Replacing it would require the agreed validation and deployment process; training must never loosen the central risk controls.

I would retain this under proposed v2.0, with account connection, activation permissions, broker reconciliation, and recovery treated as release requirements. The current v1.1 remains demo-only; this discussion does not enable real-money trading.

I'll implement all these versions, one at a time for testing over the next 2-3 days, starting today. Keep a record of completed implementations and my feedback on each until all are complete. If my feedback creates a need for further updates to be added, confirm with me the inclusion of the new feature, requirement or update, and then add it to list to track. Provide daily reminders until complete. Once complete, provide a report and recommendations based on all the implementations, my comments, etc.

Done. I’ve created the 
implementation and feedback tracker and scheduled daily evening reminders around 7 PM, starting today, until completion.

I’ll record each version’s implementation, test results, your feedback, outstanding issues, and acceptance. Any new feature or requirement arising from feedback will need your confirmation before joining the approved backlog.

Once completion is confirmed, I’ll produce the consolidated report and recommendations, then stop the reminders.

Starting position: v1.1 is delivered, your testing feedback is pending, and v1.2 is next. Share results here as you test—I cannot automatically observe your PC.

deriv-implementation-tracker.md
Document
The web-app should allow the user to setup an account using their verified email and password, or via their Gmail or Github accounts. If possible, two-factor should be enabled. If this authentication/security feature can be toggled on/off in the developer version for testing then that would be great as well.

The user should also be able to personalize their personal account in an active session once logged in. Information, such as display image, name, username, etc.

Yes—these are feasible and should be introduced before Bot Manager, because bots, trading histories, and Deriv connections will need clear account ownership.

I’ve recorded this as CR-001: Accounts, Security & Profiles, pending your confirmation.

Feature	Proposed behaviour
Email/password registration	Verify email through an expiring link; support secure password storage and password recovery.
Google/Gmail sign-in	Sign in through Google without granting access to the user’s inbox.
GitHub sign-in	Authorize through GitHub without requesting repository access.
Two-factor authentication	Authenticator-app codes with single-use recovery codes.
Profile personalization	Edit profile image, name/display name, and unique username while logged in; changes persist immediately.
Account management	Change password, reverify a changed email, manage connected sign-in methods, and sign out.
Data separation	Keep each user’s bots, trading/training records, settings, and Deriv credentials separate.

Google provides email-verification claims, while GitHub exposes verified-email information through its email API. We should check that information rather than assume every social-login account has supplied a usable verified email.

For two-factor authentication, I recommend making it optional during paper-demo testing and required before real-money activation. Changing or recovering security settings should require appropriate identity checks. Email verification and two-factor authentication remain separate steps.

The developer toggle is also feasible, with these boundaries:

Controlled through developer startup configuration.
Restricted to local development and a separate test identity/database.
Clearly labelled “Development mode—authentication bypassed.”
Real-money trading disabled while bypassed.
Production refuses to start with authentication bypass enabled.

Turning the bypass off would let you test the complete registration, sign-in, verification, and two-factor flows.

Profile edits should preserve a stable internal account identity: changing a username must not change ownership of bots, purchases, or trading records. Linking Google or GitHub to an existing account should require authentication to that account.

Confirm adding “Accounts, Security & Profiles” as a dedicated release before Bot Manager, including the developer-only authentication toggle? This confirmation follows your instruction to approve new requirements before they enter the tracked roadmap.

When a user starts a trading session...

Is the trading data saved?
If yes, where is the trading data saved for later retrieval?

Yes. In the or a complete backup, stop the Python app and copy that entire folder. Account-based separation and automatic cloud synchronization are not implemented yet.

General comment based on the overall project. Let's discuss:I want the user to be able to download the trading data for their own account. They specify the date period since their first session to the most recent/active session. They have the option to download it as csv or xlsx.

When a user creates their own bot, it saves as a file on their local machine in downloads. After each trading session or training session, the user is prompted to save the changes to their bot. If not, the changes are lost. Only the admin user can create ad save public bots that are available to all users of the system. These public bots are stored on the server. All user-specific bots, and data are private and stored as local files in the user's downloads. Only the market specific configs are saved for each user on the server. As the configs are made by the user and saved on the site, the data is updated on the database for custom/dynamic user experience. Otherwise, the user upload their bot files, training files or configuration files when needed. The user should be able to download the current configuration or training data for their select markets. They will have the option to download the full bot, the config file only or the trading data only for their live session. The data is written to the user's temporary storage locally if not explicitly downloaded.

Yes—this is feasible as a local-first design. Personal bots and trading datasets would stay on the user’s device, while the server manages public bots and saved account settings.

However, two requirements need reconciling: exporting history from the first session and discarding data that was never explicitly downloaded.

Proposed storage arrangement

Information	Proposed location
Admin-published public bots	Server
User’s saved market configurations	Server, associated with their account
Account/profile/security records	Server, if the earlier authentication proposal is approved
Personal bot files and learned models	User-selected local files
Private trading and training datasets	Local device; downloadable as CSV/XLSX
Unsaved bot changes	Local working storage
Live-trading recovery information	Automatically saved locally—recommended exception discussed below

Private file imports should be processed on the device or by the local Python companion. Sending them to a hosted training backend would change this privacy model, even if the server deleted them afterward.

Downloads and file saving

The app can offer these actions for selected markets:

Action	Output
Download full bot	.tbot: algorithm/model, configuration, version and provenance
Download configuration	.tsetup: compatible configuration values
Download training update	.ttrain: learned changes and evaluation metadata
Download trading data	CSV or XLSX for the chosen period
Download training dataset	CSV; optionally XLSX for inspection

A browser cannot silently choose and write arbitrary files in Downloads. Normal downloads follow the browser’s download settings. Supported browsers can also let the user explicitly select a file or folder and grant access for subsequent saves. We should support both approaches.

Therefore, I would describe the destination as “Downloads or a location chosen by the user.”

Saving or discarding bot changes

At the end of a trading or training session, present:

Save new bot version — preserve the updated model and configuration.
Discard bot changes — keep the previously saved version.
Return to session — postpone the decision.

These choices should apply to algorithm/model changes. Exporting the session’s trading data should remain a separate option.

Also distinguish Save configuration to my account from Save bot file. If the user explicitly saves settings to the server, subsequently discarding a bot draft should not imply those server settings were undone.

An imported bot whose settings differ from saved account settings should trigger a comparison rather than silently merging them.

We cannot guarantee a save prompt when the browser crashes or is forcibly closed. The reliable approach is an explicit End Session action, supplemented by an unsaved-changes warning where supported.

Date-range exports need retained history

The export screen can provide:

Earliest available record through the current session.
Custom start/end dates.
Account, market, bot/version, and demo/live filters.
CSV or XLSX.
An export timestamp, with open trades clearly distinguished from settled trades.

But if yesterday’s undownloaded data was discarded, the app cannot export it today. Signing in on another device would retrieve server settings—not private files that were never uploaded.

The app must therefore identify missing periods and allow users to import earlier datasets. It should never label an incomplete export “complete account history.”

Temporary local storage needs an explicit policy

Browser-managed storage can hold working data, but it is separate from Downloads. It is subject to storage limits and can be deleted when site data is cleared; it does not automatically become temporary merely because the app calls it temporary.

We should define when drafts are deleted, whether interrupted sessions can be recovered, and how different signed-in users are separated on a shared device. Local files are private from the service, but are not automatically encrypted or inaccessible to other computer users.

Live-trading recovery should survive “Discard changes”

For eventual real-money trading, I recommend a small, automatically saved local recovery record containing the cycle authorization, protective floor, and pending/open order references.

Discarding training changes must not:

Reset the protective floor.
Forget an outstanding order.
Start a new cycle with fresh loss capacity.
Treat real positions as though they disappeared.

If recovery information is unavailable, the app should block new entries until exposure and authorization can be reconciled.

Public bots and the marketplace

Admin-only publication is straightforward: only an authorized administrator can create or update the server’s public bot catalogue.

A user editing a public bot would create a private local copy. This changes the earlier marketplace proposal: users could export files for permitted private distribution, but could not directly publish server-hosted public bots. Resale permissions would still depend on the package licence.

I’ve recorded this discussion as CR-002, pending clarification and approval. It affects the data foundation, portable files, hosting, live recovery, and marketplace design.

Which history policy do you prefer before we finalize it?

A — Retain private local history automatically: supports exports back to the first retained session; saving changes to the bot file still requires confirmation.
B — Strictly discard undownloaded session data: exports cover only the current data and earlier files the user imports; complete historical retrieval cannot be guaranteed.

I recommend A, with private local history kept separate from explicitly saved bot versions.

A

Confirmed—Option A is recorded. The planned behaviour is:

Automatically retain private trading/training history locally.
Export retained history by date range as CSV or XLSX.
Require explicit saving of bot/model changes.
Discarding bot changes preserves trading history.

The 
tracker is updated; this change has not yet been implemented.

