These are my additional ideas for the store. Let's discuss before approving:
Users can sell their bots directly to the store. If bought, customers can give the purchase a nick-name and personal description for their purposes. They will then be able to post their success rates with the push of a button directly from their session to the item in the store, with comments. Other users will be able to use it to justify the posted seller success rate. Also, customers can choose a purchased compatible bot for the specific market from a list of drop-downs when uploading a trading bot to use.

These ideas are feasible and fit an admin-controlled store. I’ve recorded them as CR-003 for discussion only, without adding them to the approved roadmap.

The main distinction is between purchasing a bot, personalizing its name, modifying its behaviour, and reporting its results.

1. Users sell bots to the store

I interpret this as the store acquiring a bot or resale licence, then selling it to customers:

Creator submits a bot and supporting performance information.
Store reviews compatibility, functionality, ownership/licensing, and evidence.
Store accepts or declines the proposed purchase.
An administrator publishes an accepted version.
Customers purchase access to that version.

This preserves your earlier rule that only administrators publish public bots.

The acquisition terms need defining: buying a file, purchasing resale rights, and acquiring exclusive ownership are different arrangements.

2. Customers personalize purchased bots

A buyer could assign:

A private nickname.
A personal description.
Optional private notes.

These should leave the public item’s identity, author, and version unchanged.

Public store item	Buyer’s private label
EUR/USD Session Trader — v1.2	“My morning EUR bot”
Seller’s description	“Use during my selected morning session”

Renaming a purchase would not change its performance identity. Changing its algorithm or performance-relevant configuration would create a customized version with separately attributed results.

For your local-first model, I recommend keeping these private labels locally initially. Synchronizing them across devices would require another permitted server-side preference.

3. Publish session results to the store item

A Post Results button is practical. It should calculate results automatically and open a preview before publication.

A performance post should contain:

Information	Why it matters
Bot and configuration version	Identifies what actually ran
Market, product, and duration	Makes comparisons meaningful
Historical, paper-demo, broker-demo, or live	Distinguishes the evidence
Session dates and reporting timestamp	Defines the period
Settled trades and wins/losses/break-even	Gives the percentage context
Net trading profit/loss and measured drawdown	Shows more than win rate
Original or customized setup	Separates results from modified versions
Customer comments	Explains their experience

If posted during an active session, it should say “Session in progress—as of [time]”. Open positions must not count as settled wins.

Repeatedly pressing Post Results should update the same report or create a linked revision—not count the same trades again.

4. Customer reports can help assess seller claims—but cannot prove them

The store could show three separate views:

Seller-reported performance.
Community-submitted performance.
The buyer’s own local performance.

For example, buyers could see whether customers using the same version and original configuration report broadly similar results.

However, these badges must remain distinct:

Badge	What it establishes
Verified purchaser	The account purchased the item
App-reported results	The application supplied calculated figures
Broker-reconciled outcomes	Submitted execution/outcome records were matched against broker information

A verified purchase does not verify performance. Locally editable files can be manipulated, and even broker-confirmed trades do not independently prove which algorithm generated them.

Community results also represent people who chose to publish, not necessarily every customer. Aggregates should identify participant and trade counts, separate comparable versions/modes, and include losing results. Platform guidance likewise emphasizes collecting and moderating reviews without improperly favouring positive feedback.

5. Select purchased bots from compatible drop-downs

The market’s trader panel could offer:

Selection	Available choices
Bot source	Default bot / My purchased bots / Import local bot
Bot	Compatible purchases for that market
Version	Available compatible versions
Configuration	Original setup / saved account settings / imported setup

The app should filter by market, product, runtime compatibility, and purchase entitlement. Unsupported items could remain visible with an explanation.

Selecting a purchased bot can load the original store package. If the user wants their privately trained or customized copy, they must provide that local file. The store cannot recover private changes that were never uploaded.

Thhange based on recent user experiences, the changes will need to be approved by an admin each week, reducing bot updates to approved changes weekly. 

When a purchase is made, the user gets an 80% commission.

This changes the store into a commission-based marketplace, with two distinct bot categories. I’ve recorded the revision under the pending store proposal; it is not implemented or added to the approved roadmap yet.

Category	Access	Publication and updates
Paid seller bots	Purchase required; USD $30–$50	Product generated from an active trading session
Shared members-only bots	Available to eligible members	Changes reviewed and approved by an administrator for weekly release

Session-generated paid listings

The proposed flow would be:

User runs a trading session.
Add to Store becomes available.
The app generates the product from the current bot, configuration, market, performance, and observed market conditions.
The user sets a price between $30 and $50 USD and adds description text.
Publishing uploads that specific bot version as a purchase-protected package.

The generated information should include the trade count, reporting period, demo/live mode, bot version, and training status alongside its success rate. An active-session result should carry an “as of” timestamp.

The user would not manually edit the calculated success rate. A new session with one winning trade must remain distinguishable from a validated model with hundreds of trades.

Freeze the version being sold

The listing should capture a fixed bot/model/configuration snapshot. Continuing to train the local bot would not silently change what customers purchase.

This also keeps customer performance reports attributable to the correct version. Uploading a listing would be an explicit exception to the private-local-storage policy—not permission to upload the seller’s entire history or other bots.

Weekly updates for shared bots

Recent user experiences can inform candidate changes, but should not directly modify the shared bot. I recommend:

Collect feedback and candidate improvements.
Evaluate them before the weekly review.
Let an administrator approve, reject, or defer each change.
Publish approved changes as a new version.
Keep running sessions on their starting version.

I’m interpreting your weekly restriction as applying to shared members-only bots. The approval policy for updates to paid seller bots remains to be decided.

Seller commission

If the 80% is calculated from the listed sale price, the split would be:

Sale price	Seller: 80%	Platform remainder: 20%
$30	$24	$6
$40	$32	$8
$50	$40	$10

These figures are before deciding who bears payment fees, refunds, taxes, or other deductions. Commission earned and commission available for payout should also be tracked separately.

The first commercial detail to confirm: should the seller receive 80% of the gross sale price, or 80% of net proceeds after specified payment fees?

80% of net price

Confirmed and recorded:

Seller: 80% of the net sale price.
Platform: remaining 20% of the same net price.

The deductions that define “net price” still need agreement before payment implementation.

