When a member uses a public members-only bot, it does not provide a resale option. Also, when a user does not modify a public members-only bot, the option to download does not appear. Only custom bots provide those options. 

The store should also categorize bots and allow for filtering.

Additional ideas and features to discuss:
The app will keep a record of all trained user bots that have hit over 90% success rate. It will record the user, the bots success rate, the bots name, if the bot is in store and the specific market it has achieved the success in. Users will be able to filter the leaderboard by market, country/region and apps that are for sale. The app will also assign the user with the successful bot a special title in the app, specific to the marketplace, and put them on a leader board to highlight their success. Users can obtain multiple titles. This means the app needs to include a leaderboard to show users and their country/region who have created bots that hit or pass the 90% mark during their trading session on demo or live. The public bots will also be named and ranked on the leader board.

Guests will not be able to trade live or customize the basic public bots for demo trading. They will only be able to use the basic public bots in the various marketplaces. 

All signed in users are able to use the basic public bots, customize the basic public bots for their own use (whether in live or demo trading), and build a bot from scratch. They will have download and upload limits for their custom bots, however, of 1 download per marketplace per day and 1 upload per day regardless of marketplace. The best place to implement this limitation may be at the browser level for efficient use of server resources. They limit also applies to the configuration file. They have unlimited access to their trading data while the browser's storage still holds the data.

If a user registers for the monthly/annual membership subscription, they will have access to basic public bots (that do not undergo training more than once annually), automatically-trained members-only public bots, unlimited downloads of their customized bots/configurations/trading data, unlimited uploads, a membership badge icon beside their names in the app and tiered max cycle lengths per month. Basic Bronze members can have a max cycle length of 7 days, Silver members can have a max cycle length of 14 days, Gold members can have a max cycle length of 21 days, and Platinum members can have a maximum cycle length of 28 days. These are monthly allotments that do not roll over. Cancelled sessions still get deducted from the monthly allotment as a minimum of 1 day up to the number of days the session ran for (end date minus start date).

Users will be able to pay for each download and upload at $2.50 per download/upload. The uploads do not persist across sessions. Guest User sessions expire after 1 hour of no user interaction. They will have to sign in again and re-upload custom bot at a cost each time they want to use their downloaded custom bot if session expires or they signed out. Also, they can only trade for a maximum of 2 cycle lengths (2 days) per month. 

Users can pay for each additional day after cycle lengths run out - at $0.5 per day, minimum of 2 days required. 

Subscribed members with customize public members-only bots - once the customized app achieves a greater success rate than what is in the server for that members-only bot, then the bot data/config/algorithm will be automatically updated on the server for all future members to benefit from, making the bots always dynamic and keeping the latest and best configurations with the latest and best success rates.

These features are feasible, but they introduce a substantial membership, billing, and permissions layer. I’ve recorded them as CR-004 for discussion—not yet approved or implemented.

I’m interpreting “per marketplace” as per trading market, such as EUR/USD or V100–1s, unless you mean separate stores.

Bot categories and permissions

The catalogue now needs three principal categories:

Category	Availability	Download/resale behaviour
Basic public bots	Guests and signed-in users	Original public version has no download/resale option; trained at most annually
Members-only public bots	Subscribers	Unmodified original has no download/resale option; improvements follow the agreed publication process
Paid custom bots	Customers who purchase them	Access and permitted use follow their licence and the user’s plan

A meaningful algorithm/configuration change should create a custom derivative, retaining its parent bot and version. A nickname alone should not unlock resale.

Removing download buttons is an application permission rule—not absolute copy protection for algorithms executing on someone’s device.

Store filters could include market, category, compatible product, price, training status, demo/live evidence, success rate with trade count, and whether the bot is for sale.

Access rules need a clear distinction between guests and registered free users

The cleanest interpretation is:

Capability	Guest	Registered free user	Subscriber
Use basic public bots in demo	Yes	Yes	Yes
Customize bots/build a bot	No	Yes	Yes
Use members-only public bots	No	No	Yes
Trade live	No	After separate authorization	After separate authorization
Custom bot/config downloads	None	One per market daily	Unlimited
Custom bot/config uploads	None	One daily across markets	Unlimited
Access/export retained personal trading data	Demo records available locally	Unlimited	Unlimited

For free users, I suggest bot and configuration files share the same allowance, rather than providing separate allowances for each file type.

The $2.50 charge would apply to additional eligible file operations, after included allowances are exhausted. It should not silently apply to subscribers’ unlimited operations or to trading-data exports.

Your statement about guests signing in again and paying to re-upload a custom bot conflicts with guests being unable to use custom bots. That appears better suited to registered free users.

Browser-only quota enforcement would be unreliable

Clearing browser storage, changing browsers, or modifying client code could reset or bypass browser counters. Prices, permissions, and billable allowances should have an authoritative server-side check; OWASP explicitly recommends deriving such business-critical values server-side.

We can still minimize server resources:

Keep bot files and private datasets local.
Store a small account usage ledger on the server.
Cache remaining allowances in the browser.
Avoid duplicate charges for retries or failed operations.

These controls govern the app’s services; they cannot prevent people copying exported files outside the app.

Monthly day allowances need separating from cycle duration

Your proposed figures are:

Membership	Proposed monthly day figure
Bronze	7 days
Silver	14 days
Gold	21 days
Platinum	28 days

I recommend interpreting these as total account-wide trading-day budgets, with a separate maximum duration for one cycle.

For example, Bronze could use one seven-day cycle or several shorter cycles totalling seven days. Running five markets simultaneously would consume one account-day, rather than five.

For early cancellation, a clear proposed calculation is:

Charged days = maximum of 1 and elapsed time rounded up to whole 24-hour days.

Thus, 29 hours consumes two days. Reserve planned days when a cycle starts, then reconcile unused days when it ends early.

Additional days would cost $0.50 each, minimum two days—$1 minimum purchase. We still need to decide whether top-ups expire, and whether they extend the maximum individual cycle or only the remaining monthly budget.

Annual subscriptions should still receive their allowance in defined monthly periods; membership prices remain unspecified.

Login sessions, trading cycles, and local files must remain separate

A one-hour inactivity expiry can be implemented, with server-side enforcement. Background price polling should not count as human interaction.

However:

Expiry must not reset a cycle’s floor or allowance.
Existing real positions cannot simply be forgotten.
Re-authentication must not create another day charge for the same cycle.
An uploaded working bot can be unloaded while Option A’s retained local history remains available.
Subscribers should not pay re-upload fees where uploads are unlimited.

That preserves the distinction between losing an active file selection and losing historical records.

Leaderboards and titles are possible—but need qualification rules

The record could include the user, bot/version, market, country/region, success rate, trade count, evidence mode, dates, and sale status.

I recommend:

Separate demo and live rankings.
Separate public-bot rankings from user-created-bot achievements.
Filters for market, country/region, and for-sale bots.
Multiple market-specific titles per user.
Optional country/region disclosure.
Historical achievements labelled separately from current performance.

I would use “at least 90%” consistently, subject to your confirmation.

The 500-trade training baseline does not establish a sufficiently large evaluation sample. A trained bot winning one session trade would display 100%, but should not immediately receive an expert-style title.

We therefore need a separate minimum evaluated-trade requirement for official leaderboard eligibility. Short-session milestones could remain visible as provisional achievements, upload apply to registered free users, with guests remaining basic-demo-only. free users receive two-day monthly allowance.
Shared-bot updates: Keep automatic candidate collection followed by weekly admin approval

Confirmed and recorded:

Policy	Agreed behavior
Monthly day budgets	Bronze 7, Silver 14, Gold 21, Platinum 28 total account-wide days, shared across markets and cycles. No rollover.
Guests	Basic public bots in demo only; no custom-bot uploads.
Registered free users	Two days per month. Paid custom-bot re-upload applies to this group.
Shared members-only bots	Automatically collect candidates; publish changes only after weekly admin approval.

These three decisions are confirmed. The remaining proposed features still await approval; no application code has changed.

