Finally, let's discuss the feasibility of these addition for these new features.
Subscription members are able to recommend new members to join and if they join, the referrers receive monthly/annual passive income from their membership. 

25% of all membership fee goes to the referrer. The remaining comes to the business. If new member says no one referred them then a priority list of members with the least number of referees is used. The one at the top is automatically assigned in the back-end as the referrer to earn passive monthly commission income. The priority list is limited to the active subscribed members with less than 4 referees linked to them. This creates a systematic chain of support for those who have not yet made a referral. If the referrer becomes inactive, the commission comes to the business instead until they become active again. A member can become active if they fail to pay their monthly subscription fee within the first 5 days of it being due. Commissions already paid out to the business due to inactivity will not be retro-actively paid to the member after late payment. they would have lost that month's commission. All subscribed members must have a dashboard to track their expected monthly commission, their number of referees, their referees' usernames, wallet/balance their referral link//QR and their referral username/code to share with others. Commission only applies to memberships not to the store. Members will have the option to withdraw from their wallet or have commissions automatically sent to their verified/confirmed bank account attached. If no verified wallet or account is attached, it will be logged in our own virtual custom wallet for later transfer to bank or external wallet.

Yes—the referral dashboard, recurring commission calculation, priority assignment and payouts are technically feasible. I recommend introducing direct referrals first, then considering automatic assignment separately. The latter changes the commercial and regulatory risk substantially.

I’ve recorded this as CR-005: Membership Referrals & Commission Payouts, pending approval.

How the commission model could work

Component	Proposed behavior
Attribution	Each paying member has one direct referrer or assigned beneficiary.
Commission	That beneficiary receives 25% of eligible subscription payments while eligible.
Business share	Remaining 75%, before business expenses and any applicable payment costs.
Scope	Membership subscriptions only. Store sales retain their separate commission rules.
Levels	One level only; no commission from referrals made by someone you referred.
Missing payout destination	Earned commission remains recorded as an amount owed until a supported destination is verified.

For example, on an eligible $20 monthly payment, the member earns $5 and the business retains $15 before costs. Subscription prices remain undecided.

We must define whether the 25% applies before or after taxes, discounts, payment fees and refunds. Your earlier 80% of net price decision applies to store sales; it does not automatically define this subscription calculation.

Automatic assignment needs particular care

The system can select active subscribers with fewer than four linked members, order them by referral count, and assign the next eligible person. Ties could use the longest time since their last assignment.

However, it cannot reliably provide four paying referrals to everyone. With 100 members, assigning each member to one beneficiary creates at most 100 relationships. Giving all 100 members four would require 400 relationships. New arrivals also become eligible beneficiaries.

Consequently, this should never be advertised as guaranteed referrals, guaranteed subscription recovery or guaranteed passive income.

Requiring a paid subscription to qualify for recurring rewards—especially rewards from automatically assigned subscribers—also deserves legal review. US FTC guidance examines recruitment incentives and purchases made to maintain compensation eligibility; having a real service does not automatically resolve those concerns. That is relevant to US expansion, not a determination of Jamaican legality.

My recommendation is to:

Launch genuine direct-referral commissions first.
Consider allowing affiliate participation independently of buying a subscription.
Review automatic allocation with Jamaican counsel and the selected payment provider before offering it.

If retained, call an automatically assigned recipient a commission beneficiary, rather than claiming they actually referred that customer.

Inactivity and annual payments need precise rules

I assume “become active if they fail to pay” means become inactive after remaining unpaid beyond the five-day grace period.

Your intended rule can be implemented: commissions arising during an ineligible period go to the business, and later renewal does not recover them. But already-earned, payable commissions should remain separately recorded; inactivity should not silently erase those balances.

Annual subscriptions introduce another decision. Paying the entire annual commission immediately makes later inactivity and refunds harder to handle. I recommend monthly vesting of annual commissions, with the dashboard distinguishing expected income from earned and withdrawable income.

The terms must also specify when eligibility is checked: at the customer’s payment, at monthly vesting, or another defined cutoff.

Use a commission balance initially

An internal commission ledger is feasible and should be stored on the server. It should distinguish:

Balance	Meaning
Expected	Forecast from current subscriptions; not guaranteed
Pending	Recorded but awaiting settlement or release conditions
Available	Earned and eligible for withdrawal
Processing	A payout has been initiated
Paid/reversed	Completed payments or refund adjustments

A missing bank account does not turn an earned commission into business income—it remains an unpaid obligation.

I recommend starting with commission earnings and withdrawals only, without deposits, transfers between members or holding trading capital. Calling something a “virtual wallet” does not settle its regulatory classification. BOJ identifies payment service providers as authorised entities providing electronic payment and funds-transfer services, so the actual functionality and payout arrangement need review.

Bank and external-wallet payouts depend on a provider supporting your business, recipient countries, currencies and verification requirements. We have not selected one yet.

The dashboard can include all requested referral tools. Referees’ usernames should have clear disclosure, while their banking details, email addresses and trading information remain private.

Before approving this addition, the three main decisions are:

Launch scope: direct referrals first, or retain automatic assignment for later reviewed implementation?
Grace period: confirm that unpaid members become inactive after five days.
Commission calculation: what deductions define the eligible fee, and should annual commissions vest monthly?

