deriv-implementation-tracker.md
Document

Direct referrals first.
Unpaid members become inactive after five days.
Membership fees are collected at the start of each month. Commissions are paid 5 days after the end of the month so that refund period can be fully settled. Eligible fees then becomes 25% of the gross monthly fee, instead of the net. Annual commission should vest monthly.

If these not solve all concerns, suggest best solution for each point.

These decisions resolve the main structural questions. The remaining concerns are refund exposure, exact eligibility timing, and payout operations. I’ve recorded your decisions as confirmed; the recommendations below remain proposals.

Confirmed policy	Behavior
Referral model	Direct referrals first; automatic assignment deferred
Inactivity	Unpaid members become inactive after five days
Monthly billing	Fees collected at the start of each month
Commission	25% of the gross monthly membership fee
Payment schedule	Five days after month-end
Annual memberships	Commission vests monthly
Scope	Memberships only; store commissions remain separate

1. Five days after month-end does not eliminate refund risk

It provides a useful processing buffer, but card disputes can arrive much later. Stripe documents typical dispute windows of 120 days, sometimes longer. This illustrates the risk; we have not selected Stripe as your provider.

Recommended solution: Keep your payout schedule, hold disputed payments, and reverse the corresponding commission when a payment is refunded or charged back. Disclosed reversals can offset future commissions. Maintain a business-funded reserve for amounts already paid out.

2. Define “gross fee” precisely

My recommendation is the subscription amount actually charged after discounts, before processing fees, excluding separately collected taxes. Refunds reduce the corresponding commission proportionately.

Example	Commission
$20 monthly subscription, plus applicable tax	$5
Discounted monthly subscription charged at $16	$4
$240 annual subscription	$5 vested monthly; $60 across twelve months

The business absorbs processing costs from its share. Annual commission should use the actual annual subscription price, including any annual discount.

3. Make billing and inactivity dates unambiguous

I recommend using Jamaica time throughout:

Renewal due on the 1st.
Grace period through 11:59 PM on the 5th.
Inactive from 12:00 AM on the 6th if still unpaid.
Prior-month commissions released and payouts initiated on the 5th of the following month. Bank arrival depends on processing times.

For members joining midmonth, charge a clearly disclosed prorated first month, then renew on the 1st. Annual subscribers remain active throughout their prepaid term without monthly payment requirements.

4. Separate earned commissions from future eligibility

To preserve your intended forfeiture rule, I recommend:

Missing the renewal cutoff forfeits that month’s commission eligibility.
Late payment restores membership access but does not recover that forfeited month.
Eligibility resumes in the next qualifying month.
Previously earned commissions remain payable, even if the member becomes inactive before payout.

For example, someone who earned October commission should still receive it on November 5. Missing November’s renewal should affect November eligibility, rather than erase October earnings.

New members joining after the monthly cutoff need an explicit first-month exception, ideally earning commissions only from their activation date.

5. Keep the initial “wallet” limited to commission accounting

Start with an internal commission balance, supported by verified bank or payment-provider payouts. Show expected, pending, available, processing, paid and reversed amounts separately.

If no verified destination exists, available earnings remain owed to the member. Before launch, define payout minimums, currencies, fees, identity verification and failed-transfer handling. These records must remain on the server and survive logout or browser-data deletion.

6. Direct referrals reduce complexity, but do not settle every legal concern

The remaining issue is requiring someone to buy membership to qualify for earnings. My preferred approach is to allow free affiliate enrollment, while paid subscriptions purchase trading-app features. This separates buying the service from earning referral commissions. It would change your proposed eligibility rule, so it requires your approval.

Qualified Jamaican legal review and payment-provider acceptance should precede launch. Referral income should be marketed as variable earnings from genuine subscription sales, with no guaranteed return.

These recommendations give you a workable foundation without changing your confirmed 25% gross commission or monthly payout schedule.

