Is it feasible to bundle all pending versions into a single release in order to jump to version 2.2 with all change requests implemented?

Yes—a single BotForge27 v2.2 release is feasible. We can consolidate the remaining roadmap and change requests into one release, while still building and testing its components incrementally.

However, this is now a substantial platform build beyond the local v1.2 demo. Completing and validating the full scope within the original 2–3-day window is not realistic.

Workstream	Consolidated scope
Trading and bots	Bot Manager, granular configurations, training, backtesting, portable files, additional contract adapters and controlled live trading
Accounts and administration	Authentication, MFA, profiles, employee roles, audited member access, tasks, inbox, follow-ups and escalations
Commerce	Memberships, allowances, bot store, commissions, referrals, financial records, disputes and payouts
Community and content	Leaderboards, titles, creator channels, uploads/recording, external embeds and paid content
Experience and operations	Landing page, content management, responsive design, test accounts, monitoring and recovery

The main architectural change

The current local application cannot independently provide shared memberships, employee messaging, marketplace purchases and creator subscriptions across devices.

The consolidated platform needs:

Hosted services for accounts, permissions, public assets, billing, messages and administrative records.
A local execution/storage component to preserve your private-bot and trading-data requirements.
External integrations for authentication, email, payments, payouts, video delivery and Deriv.

It can feel like one product, but it will have multiple coordinated components.

My recommended release approach

Freeze the v2.2 scope and consolidate the approved requirements into one specification.
Build working modules in dependency order, integrating them continuously.
Deliver v2.2 release candidates for your testing, with simulated services clearly distinguished from genuine integrations.
Publish v2.2 stable after the agreed features and integrations pass acceptance.

This avoids requiring you to install every intermediate numbered version while preserving meaningful testing.

Decisions still needed before full production completion

Membership prices and bot resale/derivative licences.
Payment/payout providers, supported countries, fees, refund rules and approval thresholds.
Creator revenue split and clarification of guest paid-channel subscriptions.
Hosting, media limits and remaining operational policies.

These decisions need not prevent development of the underlying modules.

My recommendation: consolidate the target into v2.2, retain v1.2 as the testing baseline, and use staged release candidates rather than one untested final delivery. A delivery estimate should follow the consolidated architecture and integration assessment.

walk me through the decisions I need to make, with explanations and clickable options where possible, then let's finish the v2.2 build. Once done, let's set realistic timelines for each deliverable.

